using System;
using JCass_ModelCore.Models;

namespace {{NAMESPACE}};

/// <summary>
/// Stage 4a of 6: how an element changes over one period when nothing is done to it.
///
/// <para>This is the deterioration model, and it runs for the great majority of elements in every
/// period - the optimiser can only ever afford to treat a few. Everything the forecast says about
/// a do-nothing future comes from this file.</para>
///
/// <para><b>Keep the order of the updates the same as the order of the parameters on the bundle's
/// <c>parameters</c> sheet.</b> It costs nothing and it makes the two readable side by side, which
/// is how you notice that one of them is not being incremented at all.</para>
///
/// <para><b>Watch for values that depend on each other.</b> If condition is a function of age, and
/// age is incremented first, condition is computed off the new age; increment them the other way
/// round and it is computed off the old one. Both are defensible; only one is what you meant.
/// Write down which, in a comment, because it is invisible in the numbers.</para>
///
/// <para>Every rate in here is tunable by definition - deterioration rates are the first thing a
/// modeller recalibrates when observed condition data arrives. They belong in
/// <c>lookups.xlsx</c>, read through <see cref="Constants"/>. A set of them that gets refitted
/// together - regression coefficients, a per-material rate table with more than a handful of rows
/// - belongs in a CSV in the client's <c>supporting\</c> folder instead, loaded once in
/// <see cref="{{MODEL}}.SetupInstance"/>.</para>
/// </summary>
public class Incrementer
{
    private readonly ModelBase _frameworkModel;
    private readonly {{MODEL}} _domainModel;

    /// <summary>
    /// Creates the incrementer. Built once in <see cref="{{MODEL}}.SetupInstance"/>.
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="frameworkModel">The framework model, for lookups and the random source.</param>
    /// <param name="domainModel">This domain model, for its <see cref="Constants"/>.</param>
    public Incrementer(ModelBase frameworkModel, {{MODEL}} domainModel)
    {
        _frameworkModel = frameworkModel ?? throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(frameworkModel));
        _domainModel = domainModel ?? throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(domainModel));
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Advances one element by a single period, with no treatment applied. Returns the same
    /// instance, mutated - the caller writes it straight back through the parameter sinks.
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="element">The element to advance.</param>
    /// <param name="period">Modelling period (1-based) being entered.</param>
    public {{ELEMENT}} Increment({{ELEMENT}} element, int period)
    {
        // Advance each piece of state by one period, in the same order as the bundle's
        // `parameters` sheet, e.g.
        //
        //   element.Age += 1;
        //   element.ConditionRating += _domainModel.Constants.ConditionPointsPerYear;
        //
        // The rate comes from Constants, which read it from lookups.xlsx. Nothing in this file
        // should be a numeric literal that a modeller would ever want to change.

        return element;
    }
}
